window.CATALOG = {"positions":[{"id":"coach","title":"EMS Coach","icon":"bolt","description":"Runs safe, effective EMS sessions and helps clients reach their goals."},{"id":"desk","title":"Front Desk & Sales","icon":"chat","description":"Creates great first impressions, guides trial guests and keeps members engaged."},{"id":"manager","title":"Studio Manager","icon":"chart","description":"Runs daily operations, leads the team and grows the studio."}],"levels":{"1":"Foundation","2":"Practice","3":"Expert"},"passMark":90,"answerPass":60,"courses":[{"id":"coach-1","position":"coach","level":1,"title":"EMS Foundations","summary":"How EMS works, who may train, and how to run a safe session from start to finish.","minutes":60,"modules":[{"id":"basics","title":"EMS Fundamentals","icon":"bolt","summary":"What EMS is, how it works in the body, and the equipment you will use every day.","lessons":[{"id":"what","title":"What is EMS training?","html":"\n<p><strong>EMS</strong> stands for <strong>Electrical Muscle Stimulation</strong>. In <strong>whole-body EMS (WB-EMS)</strong>, the client wears a vest and cuffs with built-in electrodes. These send low-frequency electrical impulses to the major muscle groups while the client does simple exercises.</p>\n<p>Normally, the brain sends a signal through the nerves to make a muscle contract. EMS adds an <strong>external impulse</strong> through the skin. The muscle contracts more strongly, and many muscle groups work at the same time.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A typical session lasts about <strong>20 minutes</strong>.</li>\n  <li>The client does easy, guided movements, such as squats, lunges or arm pulls, while the impulse is active.</li>\n  <li>Most programmes alternate an <strong>impulse phase</strong> with a <strong>rest phase</strong>, for example 4 seconds on and 4 seconds off.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"note\">EMS is very intense even though it feels short. That is why safety, supervision and correct intensity are the core of your job as a trainer.</div>"},{"id":"body","title":"What happens in the body","html":"\n<p>Because the impulse reaches the muscle directly, EMS can also activate <strong>deep muscle fibres</strong> that are hard to reach with normal training. Agonists and antagonists (for example abdominals and lower back) can work together.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Muscle strength and tone</strong> improve with regular training.</li>\n  <li>The load on <strong>joints</strong> is low, because no heavy weights are used.</li>\n  <li>The muscles need <strong>recovery time</strong>. Training too often or too hard can overload them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Overload is a real risk with EMS. In rare cases, very intense sessions, especially for beginners, can damage muscle tissue and strain the kidneys (<strong>rhabdomyolysis</strong>). You prevent this with a slow progression, enough water and enough rest between sessions.</p>"},{"id":"equipment","title":"Our equipment","html":"\n<p>At CS FITNESS every training station has three parts:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The control unit</strong> on top of the column. It has a touch screen for programmes and timing, one dial per muscle group, and a <strong>master dial</strong> that changes all channels at once.</li>\n  <li><strong>The column with handlebar.</strong> The client can hold the handlebar for balance. The cable that connects the suit hangs from the column.</li>\n  <li><strong>The suit:</strong> a vest plus cuffs for upper arms, glutes and thighs. Each piece has electrode pads that must be <strong>moistened</strong> before training.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Typical channels: chest, abdominals, upper back, lower back, glutes, thighs (front/back) and arms. You adjust each channel separately until the client feels an even, strong but comfortable contraction.</p>\n<div class=\"note\">Learn where the <strong>stop / pause function</strong> is on the control unit before your first session. You must be able to stop the impulse instantly at any time.</div>"},{"id":"expect","title":"Realistic expectations","html":"\n<p>Clients come with very different goals: strength, back pain prevention, body shape, sports performance or simply saving time. Be honest about what EMS can and cannot do.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Can:</strong> build strength and muscle tone, support posture, and be an efficient addition to an active lifestyle.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cannot:</strong> replace a healthy diet, cardio fitness or medical treatment. It is not a quick “fat burner” on its own.</li>\n  <li>Results take <strong>weeks</strong> of regular training, usually one session per week.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Never make medical promises (“this will cure your back pain”). If a client has health problems, the doctor decides whether EMS is suitable. When you talk about results, stick to what CS FITNESS officially communicates, and never promise specific numbers to an individual client.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"id":"b1","q":"What does EMS stand for?","options":["Electronic Movement System","Electrical Muscle Stimulation","Energy Muscle Support","Elastic Muscle Strength"]},{"id":"b2","q":"How long does a typical whole-body EMS session last?","options":["About 5 minutes","About 20 minutes","About 45 minutes","About 60 minutes"]},{"id":"b3","q":"How does EMS make a muscle contract?","options":["With heat applied to the skin","With an external electrical impulse through electrodes on the skin","With vibration plates","With magnetic fields inside the body"]},{"id":"b4","q":"What is a typical impulse pattern in many programmes?","options":["Impulse on for 20 minutes without a break","Impulse phase and rest phase alternate, e.g. 4 s on / 4 s off","Only one impulse per minute","Impulse only at the end of the session"]},{"id":"b5","q":"Why is EMS easy on the joints?","options":["Because the client lies still","Because no heavy external weights are used","Because the current heals cartilage","Because it is only done once a month"]},{"id":"b6","q":"What is rhabdomyolysis?","options":["A normal kind of muscle soreness","A breakdown of muscle tissue that can strain the kidneys","A skin rash from the electrodes","A type of EMS programme"]},{"id":"b7","q":"What must you do with the electrode pads before training?","options":["Apply body lotion","Moisten them","Warm them with a hair dryer","Nothing"]},{"id":"b8","q":"What does the master dial on the control unit do?","options":["Changes all channels at once","Switches the screen off","Only changes the arm channel","Starts the music"]},{"id":"b9","q":"A client asks: “Will EMS cure my back pain?” What is the best answer?","options":["“Yes, guaranteed after 3 sessions.”","“EMS can strengthen your back muscles, but I can’t promise a cure. Please check with your doctor first.”","“No, EMS is bad for backs.”","“Just increase the intensity and it will go away.”"]},{"id":"b10","q":"What should you know before your very first session as a trainer?","options":["The client’s favourite music","Where the stop/pause function is, so you can stop the impulse instantly","How to change the screen colour","The price of the suit"]}]},{"id":"safety","title":"Safety & Contraindications","icon":"shield","summary":"Who may train, who may not, the rules you must follow, and warning signs to watch for.","lessons":[{"id":"rules","title":"Studio rules and qualifications","html":"\n<p>Whole-body EMS is safe when it is done right, and doing it right is your job. At CS FITNESS these rules always apply:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Certified coaches only.</strong> You run sessions on your own only after you have completed this academy and the certification required by the studio and the device manufacturer.</li>\n  <li><strong>One coach, full attention.</strong> Every session is personally guided by a certified coach. A client is <strong>never left alone</strong> while the suit is connected.</li>\n  <li><strong>Health screening first.</strong> Every new client, including free trial sessions, completes a short health screening before the first session.</li>\n  <li><strong>Follow the manufacturer.</strong> Use the device, programmes, suit and cleaning exactly as the manufacturer’s manual describes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Document every session</strong> in the client file.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"note\">The safety guidance in this academy follows established industry standards for EMS studios (for example the German DIN 33961-5 standard, one of the most detailed guidelines for whole-body EMS). Your manager has the final say on studio-specific rules.</div>"},{"id":"absolute","title":"Absolute contraindications – no training","html":"\n<p>If any of these apply, the client <strong>must not</strong> do EMS training. A doctor’s note does not change this at CS FITNESS.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Pacemaker</strong>, defibrillator or other implanted electronic devices</li>\n  <li><strong>Pregnancy</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>Acute illness</strong>, fever, infection or inflammation</li>\n  <li><strong>Cardiac arrhythmia</strong> and serious heart disease</li>\n  <li>Severe <strong>circulation disorders</strong> / arteriosclerosis</li>\n  <li><strong>Cancer</strong> / tumour diseases</li>\n  <li><strong>Bleeding disorders</strong> (e.g. haemophilia)</li>\n  <li><strong>Epilepsy</strong> and severe neurological diseases</li>\n  <li><strong>Diabetes mellitus</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>Abdominal or inguinal hernia</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>Recent surgery</strong> or open wounds in the electrode area</li>\n  <li>Being under the influence of <strong>alcohol or drugs</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"note\">This list follows established EMS industry guidelines. Always check it against the manufacturer’s manual and your certification materials. <strong>When in doubt: no training.</strong></div>"},{"id":"relative","title":"Relative contraindications – doctor first","html":"\n<p>With these conditions, the client may only train after a <strong>written clearance from a doctor</strong>, and usually at a reduced intensity:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Metal <strong>implants</strong> or joint replacements (older than six months)</li>\n  <li>Diseases of the <strong>internal organs</strong>, especially the kidneys</li>\n  <li><strong>Cardiovascular disease</strong>, including high blood pressure</li>\n  <li><strong>Herniated disc</strong> and other acute back problems</li>\n  <li><strong>Oedema</strong> (large fluid retention in the body)</li>\n  <li><strong>Skin injuries</strong> or skin conditions where the electrodes sit</li>\n  <li>Strong <strong>motion sickness</strong> / dizziness</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Write the clearance into the client file and keep the doctor’s note. If the client’s health changes, they must tell you, and you update their health screening.</p>"},{"id":"warning","title":"Warning signs and emergencies","html":"\n<p><strong>During the session</strong>, stop the impulse immediately if the client:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>feels dizzy, sick, short of breath or has chest pain</li>\n  <li>turns pale, sweats unusually or seems confused</li>\n  <li>reports sharp pain, a burning feeling or a “stinging” spot on the skin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Then disconnect the cable, help the client sit or lie down, give water, and stay with them. Call <strong>911</strong> if symptoms are serious or do not improve quickly, and inform the studio manager.</p>\n<p><strong>After the session</strong>, tell every client, and especially beginners, to see a doctor immediately if they have:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>extreme muscle pain</strong> or swelling that is much worse than normal soreness</li>\n  <li><strong>dark, cola-coloured urine</strong></li>\n  <li>strong weakness or feeling unwell</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"note\">These can be signs of rhabdomyolysis. Document every incident in the client file.</div>"}],"quiz":[{"id":"s1","q":"Who may run an EMS session on their own at CS FITNESS?","options":["Any employee who has watched a session once","Only a certified coach who has completed this academy","The client, once they know the exercises","Anyone, as long as the intensity is low"]},{"id":"s2","q":"What must every new client do before their first session, including a free trial?","options":["Pay for a 10-session package","Complete a short health screening","Bring their own suit","Nothing, they can start right away"]},{"id":"s3","q":"A client has a pacemaker. What do you do?","options":["Train at low intensity","Train only the legs","No EMS training – absolute contraindication","Ask the client to sign a waiver"]},{"id":"s4","q":"A client tells you she is pregnant. What applies?","options":["Training is fine in the first three months","No EMS training","Training only with a doctor’s note","Only the arm channels may be used"]},{"id":"s5","q":"A client had a hip replacement 2 years ago. What do you do?","options":["Refuse forever","Train only after written clearance from a doctor","Train normally, no need to ask","Increase intensity on the hip"]},{"id":"s6","q":"A client arrives with a cold and a slight fever. What do you do?","options":["Train at half intensity","Cancel and reschedule – acute illness/fever means no training","Train but give extra water","Let them decide"]},{"id":"s7","q":"During the session the client says they feel dizzy and sick. What is your first step?","options":["Ask them to push through the last minutes","Stop the impulse immediately","Lower the arms channel","Give them a snack and continue"]},{"id":"s8","q":"Which symptom after training is a reason to see a doctor immediately?","options":["Mild muscle soreness the next day","Dark, cola-coloured urine","Feeling hungry","Feeling motivated"]},{"id":"s9","q":"When must a client complete the health screening?","options":["Before the first session, and again when their health changes","After 10 sessions","Only if they ask","Never, a verbal answer is enough"]},{"id":"s10","q":"You are unsure whether a condition is a contraindication. What do you do?","options":["Train anyway at low intensity","No training until it has been clarified (doctor / studio manager)","Search online and decide yourself","Let the client decide"]}]},{"id":"session","title":"Running a Session","icon":"play","summary":"Step by step from the client’s arrival to cleaning the suit afterwards.","lessons":[{"id":"before","title":"Before the session","html":"\n<ul class=\"checklist\">\n  <li>Greet the client and ask: <strong>“How are you today? Anything new with your health?”</strong></li>\n  <li>New client (or free trial): health screening completed and checked, contraindications ruled out.</li>\n  <li>The client should drink about <strong>16 oz (0.5 l) of water</strong> before training.</li>\n  <li>No hard workouts in the <strong>24 hours before</strong> an EMS session.</li>\n  <li>No heavy meal right before training, and no training on a completely empty stomach.</li>\n  <li>The client changes into the <strong>functional underwear</strong>. No jewellery, and no lotion or oil on the skin.</li>\n  <li>Check the suit: clean, no damage, electrodes intact.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"suit","title":"Suiting up","html":"\n<ul class=\"checklist\">\n  <li><strong>Moisten all electrodes</strong> evenly with water (spray bottle). Dry spots can cause an uncomfortable stinging feeling.</li>\n  <li>Put on the vest and cuffs. They should be <strong>snug</strong>, with full contact on the skin but not too tight.</li>\n  <li>Check the positions: the electrodes sit on the muscle belly (chest, abs, upper/lower back, glutes, thighs, arms).</li>\n  <li>Connect the cable from the column to the suit. Check that the plug is secure.</li>\n  <li>Choose the programme on the control unit and check that <strong>all channels start at zero</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"during","title":"During the session","html":"\n<p><strong>Setting the intensity</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Raise the intensity <strong>slowly, one channel at a time</strong>, during the impulse phase, and ask the client after every step.</li>\n  <li>The goal is a strong but comfortable contraction that feels even on <strong>left and right</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Use a perceived-exertion scale from 1 to 10. In the <strong>first sessions</strong>, stay around <strong>5 (“somewhat hard”)</strong>. After several weeks, clients usually train at <strong>6–7 (“hard”)</strong>, never at maximum.</li>\n  <li>The impulse feels weaker after a few minutes. Re-adjust carefully, always together with the client.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Supervision</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Stay with the client the entire time and keep eye contact.</li>\n  <li>Demonstrate and correct the exercises. Count with the client and motivate them.</li>\n  <li>Check in every few minutes: “How does that feel? Even on both sides?”</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"after","title":"After the session & how often to train","html":"\n<ul class=\"checklist\">\n  <li>Turn all channels down to zero and end the programme <strong>before</strong> disconnecting the cable.</li>\n  <li>Help the client out of the suit. Ask how they feel.</li>\n  <li>The client should drink water again. Remind beginners of the warning signs (extreme pain, dark urine).</li>\n  <li><strong>Document</strong> the session: date, programme, intensity per channel, notes.</li>\n  <li>Clean the suit and electrodes as the manufacturer requires and hang them up to dry. The functional underwear goes into the laundry.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Frequency:</strong> as a rule, <strong>one session per week</strong>. Leave at least <strong>4 days</strong> between sessions so the muscles can recover. Increase the intensity gradually over the first weeks.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"id":"r1","q":"How much water should the client drink before training?","options":["None","About 16 oz (0.5 l)","At least a gallon","Only a sip"]},{"id":"r2","q":"Why must the electrodes be moistened?","options":["To cool the client","For even impulse transmission and to avoid a stinging feeling","To clean the suit","It is optional"]},{"id":"r3","q":"What should be on the client’s skin under the suit?","options":["Body lotion for comfort","Nothing – only the functional underwear, no lotion or oil","Massage oil","Sunscreen"]},{"id":"r4","q":"Before starting the programme, all channels should be…","options":["At maximum","At zero","At the last client’s values","At 50%"]},{"id":"r5","q":"How do you raise the intensity?","options":["Turn the master dial to maximum quickly","Slowly, one channel at a time, asking the client after each step","Only at the end of the session","Let the client do it alone"]},{"id":"r6","q":"On a 1–10 exertion scale, where should a first-time client be?","options":["10 – maximum","Around 5 – somewhat hard","1 – they should feel nothing","8–9 – very hard"]},{"id":"r7","q":"The client says the left thigh feels much stronger than the right. What do you do?","options":["Ignore it","Adjust the thigh channels so both sides feel even, and check the electrode contact","Stop the session for good","Increase all channels"]},{"id":"r8","q":"May you leave the client alone for 2 minutes during the session?","options":["Yes, if the intensity is low","No – never leave a connected client alone","Yes, if another client calls you","Yes, during the rest phase"]},{"id":"r9","q":"How often should a client typically train?","options":["Every day","About once a week, with at least 4 days between sessions","Three times a week","Once a month"]},{"id":"r10","q":"What do you do after the session, before disconnecting the cable?","options":["Nothing special","Turn all channels to zero and end the programme","Raise the intensity one last time","Take off the vest first"]}]},{"id":"watch","type":"video","title":"Watch & Explain","icon":"video","summary":"Watch the explainers, then answer in your own words, typed or as a voice memo.","videos":[{"id":"v-how","title":"How EMS works","src":"","scenes":[{"type":"title","title":"How EMS works","caption":"A one-minute explainer for new coaches.","duration":5},{"type":"brain","title":"The natural signal","caption":"Normally the brain sends a signal through the nerves. The muscle receives it and contracts.","duration":8},{"type":"electrode","title":"The EMS impulse","caption":"With EMS, electrodes in the suit add an external impulse through the skin, so the muscle contracts more strongly.","duration":8},{"type":"groups","title":"All muscle groups at once","caption":"Chest, abs, back, glutes, thighs and arms work at the same time, including deep muscle fibres.","duration":8},{"type":"cycle","title":"Impulse and rest","caption":"Programmes alternate an impulse phase with a rest phase, for example 4 seconds on and 4 seconds off.","duration":9},{"type":"week","title":"20 minutes, once a week","caption":"Because it is so intense, one 20-minute session per week is enough. Muscles need at least 4 days to recover.","duration":9}],"prompts":[{"id":"p1","question":"A new client asks: “How does EMS actually work, and why is 20 minutes once a week enough?” Explain it in your own words, as you would to the client.","points":6}]},{"id":"v-prep","title":"Getting a client ready","src":"","scenes":[{"type":"title","title":"Getting a client ready","caption":"From arrival to the first impulse.","duration":5},{"type":"checklist","title":"Health check first","caption":"Ask about the client’s health every time. New clients complete the health screening before their first session, including free trials.","items":["Health screening","Any illness or fever today?","Contraindications ruled out"],"duration":9},{"type":"water","title":"Drink water","caption":"About 16 oz of water before training. No heavy meal right before, and no hard workout in the 24 hours before.","duration":8},{"type":"spray","title":"Moisten the electrodes","caption":"Spray all electrodes evenly with water. Dry spots can sting.","duration":8},{"type":"station","title":"Connect and start at zero","caption":"Put on the suit snugly, connect the cable, choose the programme and check that every channel is at zero.","duration":9},{"type":"dial","title":"Raise slowly, ask often","caption":"Raise the intensity channel by channel and ask after each step. Beginners stay around 5 out of 10.","duration":9},{"type":"chat","title":"Stay with your client","caption":"Check in every few minutes and never leave a connected client alone.","lines":[["coach","How does that feel?"],["client","Strong, but even on both sides."],["coach","Perfect. Let’s keep it there."]],"duration":9}],"prompts":[{"id":"p1","question":"Walk us through how you prepare a first-time client, from arrival until the first impulse. What do you check, and why?","points":7},{"id":"p2","question":"Ten minutes into the session, your client says they feel dizzy and a bit sick. What exactly do you do?","points":5}]}]}]},{"id":"coach-2","position":"coach","level":2,"title":"Coaching in Practice","summary":"Great exercise technique, clear cues, and a smart plan for every client’s first weeks.","minutes":45,"modules":[{"id":"exercises","title":"Exercises & Cues","icon":"bolt","summary":"The core movements of an EMS session and how to coach them.","lessons":[{"id":"principles","title":"Movement principles","html":"\n<p>During the impulse phase the muscles are already working hard. The movements themselves stay <strong>simple, controlled and joint-friendly</strong>. Your job is to make every rep count.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Move with the impulse.</strong> The client holds or performs the movement while the impulse is on and relaxes in the rest phase.</li>\n  <li><strong>Quality over range.</strong> A small, controlled movement with good posture beats a deep, wobbly one.</li>\n  <li><strong>Breathe.</strong> Remind clients not to hold their breath during the impulse.</li>\n  <li><strong>Neutral spine.</strong> Chest up, core braced, knees in line with the toes.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Simple movement + strong impulse = effective session. If technique breaks down, lower the intensity, not the standard.</div>"},{"id":"core-moves","title":"The core movements","html":"\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Squat:</strong> feet hip-width, sit back as if onto a chair, knees track over the toes, chest up. Cue: <em>“Push the floor away.”</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Lunge:</strong> long step, both knees around 90°, front knee stays behind the toes. Cue: <em>“Straight down like an elevator.”</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Row / pull:</strong> slight bend at the hips, pull the elbows back, squeeze the shoulder blades. Cue: <em>“Elbows into your back pockets.”</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Chest press:</strong> arms push forward against the impulse, shoulders down. Cue: <em>“Long arms, low shoulders.”</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Core brace / crunch:</strong> pull the belly button in, exhale during the impulse. Cue: <em>“Zip up your jeans.”</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Show each movement first, let the client try it <strong>without impulse</strong>, then add the impulse.</p>"},{"id":"cueing","title":"Cueing like a pro","html":"\n<p>Good coaches talk less and say more. Use three types of cues:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Visual:</strong> demonstrate the movement in front of the mirror.</li>\n  <li><strong>Verbal:</strong> short, positive words, one correction at a time.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tactile:</strong> a light touch on the shoulder or back, <strong>only after asking</strong> for permission.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Count the impulse phases out loud, give energy in the last seconds, and praise specific things: <em>“Great depth on that squat.”</em></p>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">One cue at a time, positive language, and ask before you touch.</div>"}]},{"id":"programming","title":"Programming & Progression","icon":"chart","summary":"How to plan the first weeks and adapt sessions to each goal.","lessons":[{"id":"first-weeks","title":"The first weeks","html":"\n<p>The first sessions decide whether a client stays. They should feel challenging but safe.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Session 1:</strong> low intensity (around 5 out of 10), learn the movements, explain the feeling.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sessions 2–4:</strong> raise the intensity step by step, only if recovery was good.</li>\n  <li><strong>From week 4–6:</strong> most clients train at 6–7 out of 10 with more demanding movements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Always ask at the start: <em>“How did you feel after the last session? Any unusual soreness?”</em> If soreness lasted more than 3 days, keep the intensity the same or lower it.</p>"},{"id":"goals","title":"Adapting to goals","html":"\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Strength & shape:</strong> slower, held positions during the impulse, more muscle groups at higher intensity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Back health:</strong> focus on core and back channels, controlled posture work, no jerky movements. Doctor’s clearance if there is a medical back problem.</li>\n  <li><strong>Weight management:</strong> EMS supports muscle and metabolism. Combine with everyday activity and nutrition, and be honest that EMS alone is not a diet.</li>\n  <li><strong>Athletes:</strong> sport-specific movements, but keep the session within the studio’s programmes.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"documenting","title":"Documenting progress","html":"\n<p>Write down after every session:</p>\n<ul class=\"checklist\">\n  <li>Programme and intensity per channel</li>\n  <li>Exercises and any changes</li>\n  <li>How the client felt during and after</li>\n  <li>Anything unusual (pain, dizziness, skin reactions)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Good notes let any coach take over the next session safely, and they show the client their progress.</p>"}]},{"id":"check","title":"Final Check","icon":"check","summary":"Ten questions on Level 2.","lessons":[],"quiz":[{"id":"c2q1","q":"When does the client perform the movement?","options":["Only in the rest phase","During the impulse phase, relaxing in the rest phase","At random","Only at the end of the session"]},{"id":"c2q2","q":"A client’s squat technique breaks down at the current intensity. What do you do?","options":["Ignore it, the impulse does the work","Lower the intensity and keep the technique standard","Raise the intensity","Skip squats forever"]},{"id":"c2q3","q":"What is a good cue for the row?","options":["“Shoulders up to your ears.”","“Elbows into your back pockets.”","“Lock your knees.”","“Hold your breath.”"]},{"id":"c2q4","q":"Before using a tactile cue (touch), you…","options":["just do it","ask the client for permission","raise the intensity","stop the session"]},{"id":"c2q5","q":"How should you introduce a new movement?","options":["Directly at full intensity","Demonstrate, let the client try it without impulse, then add the impulse","Only explain it verbally","Let the client watch a video at home"]},{"id":"c2q6","q":"What intensity is right for a client’s first session?","options":["Around 5 out of 10","9 out of 10","10 out of 10","Whatever the client wants"]},{"id":"c2q7","q":"A client had strong soreness for 4 days after the last session. What do you do?","options":["Raise the intensity","Keep the same intensity or lower it","Train twice this week","Nothing"]},{"id":"c2q8","q":"A client with a medical back problem wants to train. What applies?","options":["Train at maximum to strengthen the back","Doctor’s clearance first, then core and back focus with controlled movement","Only arms and legs","No questions needed"]},{"id":"c2q9","q":"A client wants to lose weight. What is the honest message?","options":["EMS alone melts fat","EMS supports muscle and metabolism; combine it with activity and nutrition","Diet is not important","Just train every day"]},{"id":"c2q10","q":"Why do you document every session?","options":["It is optional","So any coach can continue safely and progress is visible","Only for billing","To fill time"]}]},{"id":"watch","type":"video","title":"Watch & Explain","icon":"video","summary":"Coaching a real situation.","videos":[{"id":"v-cue","title":"Leading a great session","src":"","scenes":[{"type":"title","title":"Leading a great session","caption":"Technique, cues and energy.","duration":5},{"type":"steps","title":"Show, try, add impulse","caption":"Demonstrate the movement, let the client try it without impulse, then add the impulse.","items":["Show","Try","Impulse"],"duration":9},{"type":"chat","title":"One cue at a time","caption":"Short, positive words. Praise specific things.","lines":[["coach","Push the floor away."],["coach","Great depth on that squat!"],["client","That one burned!"]],"duration":9},{"type":"dial","title":"Progress step by step","caption":"Start around 5 out of 10. Most clients reach 6–7 after a few weeks, never the maximum.","duration":9},{"type":"chat","title":"“Make it harder!”","caption":"Some clients want maximum intensity right away. Explain why progression matters.","lines":[["client","Turn it all the way up!"],["coach","Let’s build up safely. Your muscles need to adapt first."]],"duration":9}],"prompts":[{"id":"p1","question":"It’s your client’s second session. They say: “This is too easy, turn it all the way up!” How do you respond, and what do you do?","points":5}]}]}]},{"id":"coach-3","position":"coach","level":3,"title":"Expert Coach","summary":"Special client groups, red flags, and keeping clients motivated for the long run.","minutes":45,"modules":[{"id":"special","title":"Special Clients & Red Flags","icon":"shield","summary":"Adapting EMS for different people and knowing when to refer.","lessons":[{"id":"groups","title":"Adapting for different clients","html":"\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Older adults:</strong> often great results for strength and balance. Start lower, progress slower, use support (handlebar) for balance, and allow more recovery time.</li>\n  <li><strong>Complete beginners:</strong> extra time for explanation, very gradual progression, lots of positive feedback.</li>\n  <li><strong>Desk workers with back tension:</strong> focus on posture, core and upper back. Medical back problems need a doctor’s clearance first.</li>\n  <li><strong>Athletes:</strong> high motivation. Make sure they don’t combine a maximum EMS session with hard training on the same or next day.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">The stronger the client feels, the more you have to protect them from doing too much too soon.</div>"},{"id":"red-flags","title":"Red flags: when to stop or refer","html":"\n<p>Stop the session and refer the client to a doctor if you notice:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Extreme muscle pain</strong> or swelling after a session, much worse than normal soreness</li>\n  <li><strong>Dark, cola-coloured urine</strong> (possible rhabdomyolysis, an emergency)</li>\n  <li>Chest pain, shortness of breath, irregular heartbeat, dizziness</li>\n  <li>New health information: pregnancy, new medication, surgery, a new diagnosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Never diagnose. Say: <em>“I want you to get this checked by a doctor before we continue.”</em> Then inform the manager and document it.</p>"}]},{"id":"results","title":"Results & Retention","icon":"chart","summary":"Goals, progress check-ins and motivation.","lessons":[{"id":"goals","title":"Goals that keep clients coming","html":"\n<p>Clients stay when they feel progress. Set goals that are <strong>specific and measurable</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Vague: <em>“Get fitter.”</em> Better: <em>“Carry groceries upstairs without back pain in 8 weeks.”</em></li>\n  <li>Write the goal in the client file and refer to it regularly.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"checkins","title":"Progress check-ins","html":"\n<ul>\n  <li>Every <strong>4–6 weeks</strong>: a short review of the goal, how they feel, and what changed.</li>\n  <li>Use what you can measure: body measurements, posture photos (only with consent), how exercises feel, everyday improvements.</li>\n  <li>Celebrate small wins: <em>“Remember when lunges were hard?”</em></li>\n  <li>If progress stalls, look at frequency, recovery, sleep and everyday activity, not just intensity.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">A client who sees progress tells friends. Retention and referrals start in your sessions.</div>"}]},{"id":"check","title":"Final Check","icon":"check","summary":"Ten questions on Level 3.","lessons":[],"quiz":[{"id":"c3q1","q":"How do you adapt EMS for an older adult?","options":["Same as everyone","Start lower, progress slower, offer balance support, allow more recovery","Maximum intensity for faster results","Older adults can never train"]},{"id":"c3q2","q":"An athlete plans a hard training session the day after EMS. What do you advise?","options":["Great idea","Avoid combining a maximum EMS session with hard training on the same or next day","Do EMS twice that week","Nothing"]},{"id":"c3q3","q":"Which of these is a red flag after a session?","options":["Mild soreness for 1–2 days","Dark, cola-coloured urine","Feeling motivated","Feeling hungry"]},{"id":"c3q4","q":"A client mentions new heart medication. What do you do?","options":["Continue as usual","Pause EMS until a doctor has cleared it, inform the manager, document","Lower the arms channel","Ask them to stop the medication"]},{"id":"c3q5","q":"What do you say when you suspect a health problem?","options":["A diagnosis","“I want you to get this checked by a doctor before we continue.”","Nothing","“It’s probably nothing.”"]},{"id":"c3q6","q":"Which goal is best?","options":["“Get fitter.”","“Carry groceries upstairs without back pain in 8 weeks.”","“Look better.”","“Train sometimes.”"]},{"id":"c3q7","q":"How often should you do a progress check-in?","options":["Never","Every 4–6 weeks","Every session","Once a year"]},{"id":"c3q8","q":"You want to take posture photos. What is required?","options":["Nothing","The client’s consent","A manager’s signature only","A doctor"]},{"id":"c3q9","q":"Progress stalls. What do you look at first?","options":["Only raising the intensity","Frequency, recovery, sleep and everyday activity","Changing the client’s coach","Nothing"]},{"id":"c3q10","q":"Why does retention start in your sessions?","options":["It doesn’t","Clients who feel progress stay and refer friends","Because of the music","Because of the prices"]}]},{"id":"watch","type":"video","title":"Watch & Explain","icon":"video","summary":"A difficult case.","videos":[{"id":"v-case","title":"A difficult case","src":"","scenes":[{"type":"title","title":"A difficult case","caption":"Recognise red flags and act correctly.","duration":5},{"type":"chat","title":"A worrying call","caption":"A first-time client calls two days after the session.","lines":[["client","My legs are swollen and really hurt."],["client","And my urine is very dark."]],"duration":9},{"type":"alert","title":"Red flag","caption":"Extreme pain, swelling and dark urine can be signs of rhabdomyolysis. This needs medical care right away.","label":"See a doctor now","duration":9},{"type":"checklist","title":"What you do","caption":"Send the client to medical care, inform the manager, document everything and pause training until a doctor clears it.","items":["Medical care now","Inform manager","Document","Pause training"],"duration":10}],"prompts":[{"id":"p1","question":"A first-time client calls two days after their session: their legs are swollen and very painful, and their urine is dark. What do you tell them and what do you do next?","points":6},{"id":"p2","question":"A 67-year-old client starts EMS to improve strength and balance. How do you adapt the first sessions for them?","points":6}]}]}]},{"id":"desk-1","position":"desk","level":1,"title":"Welcome & Front Desk","summary":"First impressions, explaining EMS in plain words, and running the front desk smoothly.","minutes":35,"modules":[{"id":"experience","title":"The CS FITNESS Experience","icon":"star","summary":"What we offer and how every visit should feel.","lessons":[{"id":"welcome","title":"Every visit starts with you","html":"\n<p>You are the first and last person a client sees. Within seconds they decide whether they feel welcome.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Greet within 5 seconds</strong>, with eye contact, a smile and their name if you know it.</li>\n  <li><strong>Stand up</strong> or come around the desk for new visitors.</li>\n  <li><strong>Say goodbye</strong> with something personal: <em>“Great job today, see you next week!”</em></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">People forget what you said, but they remember how you made them feel.</div>"},{"id":"what-we-do","title":"Explaining EMS in 30 seconds","html":"\n<p>Many callers and visitors have never heard of EMS. Keep it simple and honest:</p>\n<div class=\"script\">“EMS stands for Electrical Muscle Stimulation. You wear a special suit with electrodes that activate all your major muscles at once while a certified coach guides you through simple exercises. A session takes just 20 minutes, once a week, and it’s low-impact, so it’s easy on the joints. The best way to understand it is to try it in a free trial session.”</div>\n<ul>\n  <li>Our promise: <strong>Low impact. Full body.</strong></li>\n  <li>Never promise medical results or specific numbers to an individual.</li>\n  <li>For health questions, refer to the health screening and the coach.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"id":"desk","title":"Booking & Studio Basics","icon":"calendar","summary":"Phone, bookings, check-in and preparing clients.","lessons":[{"id":"phone","title":"Phone & messages","html":"\n<ul>\n  <li>Answer with the studio name and your name: <em>“CS FITNESS Green Valley, this is Sam, how can I help?”</em></li>\n  <li>Answer messages and Instagram DMs within the same business day.</li>\n  <li>Always end with a <strong>next step</strong>: a booked trial, a call-back time or a clear answer.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"booking","title":"Booking & preparing clients","html":"\n<p>When you book a session, especially a first one, tell the client:</p>\n<ul class=\"checklist\">\n  <li>Drink plenty of water before the session</li>\n  <li>No hard workout in the 24 hours before</li>\n  <li>No heavy meal right before</li>\n  <li>Arrive 10 minutes early for the first visit (health screening)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At check-in: greet, confirm the booking, make sure new clients complete the <strong>health screening</strong> before they meet the coach.</p>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">A well-prepared client has a better session, and the coach can start on time.</div>"}]},{"id":"check","title":"Final Check","icon":"check","summary":"Ten questions on Level 1.","lessons":[],"quiz":[{"id":"d1q1","q":"How quickly should you greet someone who walks in?","options":["Within about 5 seconds","After finishing your current task","Only if they speak first","After a minute"]},{"id":"d1q2","q":"How long is a typical CS FITNESS EMS session?","options":["5 minutes","20 minutes","60 minutes","90 minutes"]},{"id":"d1q3","q":"What is our studio’s slogan?","options":["No pain, no gain","Low impact. Full body.","Just do it","Train hard every day"]},{"id":"d1q4","q":"A caller asks if EMS will cure their back pain. You…","options":["Promise it will","Explain what EMS does and refer health questions to the screening and coach","Say EMS is dangerous","Hang up"]},{"id":"d1q5","q":"How do you answer the phone?","options":["“Hello?”","Studio name and your name, then offer help","Just your name","“Yes?”"]},{"id":"d1q6","q":"Every call or message should end with…","options":["a goodbye only","a clear next step","a price list","nothing special"]},{"id":"d1q7","q":"What should a client avoid in the 24 hours before EMS?","options":["Drinking water","A hard workout","Sleeping","Walking"]},{"id":"d1q8","q":"What must a new client complete before meeting the coach?","options":["A membership contract","The health screening","A fitness test","Nothing"]},{"id":"d1q9","q":"How soon should Instagram DMs be answered?","options":["Within a week","Within the same business day","Only on weekends","Never"]},{"id":"d1q10","q":"When should a first-time client arrive?","options":["Exactly on time","About 10 minutes early","30 minutes late","Whenever"]}]},{"id":"watch","type":"video","title":"Watch & Explain","icon":"video","summary":"The perfect first contact.","videos":[{"id":"v-first","title":"The perfect first contact","src":"","scenes":[{"type":"title","title":"The perfect first contact","caption":"Phone, door, desk: you set the tone.","duration":5},{"type":"phone","title":"Answer like a pro","caption":"Studio name, your name, and an offer to help.","label":"CS FITNESS Green Valley, this is Sam!","duration":8},{"type":"chat","title":"“What is EMS?”","caption":"Keep it simple: suit with electrodes, all major muscles, simple exercises with a coach, 20 minutes once a week, low impact.","lines":[["client","What exactly is EMS?"],["coach","A suit activates all your major muscles while a coach guides you. 20 minutes, once a week."]],"duration":10},{"type":"stat","title":"Low impact. Full body.","caption":"20 minutes per week, easy on the joints.","value":"20","unit":"min / week","duration":7},{"type":"calendar","title":"Always a next step","caption":"Offer the free trial session and book it right away.","label":"Free trial booked","duration":8}],"prompts":[{"id":"p1","question":"A caller says: “I saw your ad. What exactly is EMS, and is it for me?” Answer as you would on the phone, in about 30 seconds.","points":6}]}]}]},{"id":"desk-2","position":"desk","level":2,"title":"Trial Sessions & Consultation","summary":"Guide a guest through the free trial and lead a consultation that fits their needs.","minutes":40,"modules":[{"id":"trial","title":"The Free Trial Journey","icon":"route","summary":"From booking to the consultation after the session.","lessons":[{"id":"journey","title":"Five steps of a great trial","html":"\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n  <li><strong>Book:</strong> date, time, preparation tips (water, no hard workout 24 h before).</li>\n  <li><strong>Reminder:</strong> a friendly message the day before.</li>\n  <li><strong>Welcome & screening:</strong> greet by name, health screening, introduce the coach.</li>\n  <li><strong>Session:</strong> the coach leads the trial. You make sure everything runs on time.</li>\n  <li><strong>Consultation:</strong> right after the session, while the experience is fresh.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Most decisions are made in the 10 minutes after the trial. Never let a guest leave without a conversation.</div>"},{"id":"no-shows","title":"Reducing no-shows","html":"\n<ul>\n  <li>Book trials within the next few days, not weeks away.</li>\n  <li>Confirm by message and remind the day before.</li>\n  <li>If someone doesn’t show up: call the same day, friendly and without pressure, and offer a new time.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"id":"consult","title":"Consultation Skills","icon":"chat","summary":"Listen first, then recommend.","lessons":[{"id":"needs","title":"Needs analysis: ask, then listen","html":"\n<p>A good consultation is <strong>70% listening</strong>. Use open questions:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>“What made you look into EMS?”</em></li>\n  <li><em>“What would you like to achieve in the next 3 months?”</em></li>\n  <li><em>“What has stopped you in the past?”</em></li>\n  <li><em>“How did the session feel today?”</em></li>\n  <li><em>“How much time do you realistically have per week?”</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Repeat their goal in your words: <em>“So you want to get rid of the back tension and feel stronger, without spending hours in the gym.”</em></p>"},{"id":"recommend","title":"Recommending the right option","html":"\n<ul>\n  <li>Connect the option to <strong>their goal</strong>, not to features.</li>\n  <li>Explain options clearly and honestly, using the studio’s current offers and price list.</li>\n  <li>Ask for the decision: <em>“Shall we get your first sessions booked?”</em></li>\n  <li>If they need time, agree on a <strong>specific follow-up</strong> (day and time) and keep it.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Help people decide, never pressure them. Honest advice builds trust and referrals.</div>"}]},{"id":"check","title":"Final Check","icon":"check","summary":"Ten questions on Level 2.","lessons":[],"quiz":[{"id":"d2q1","q":"When is the best moment for the consultation?","options":["A week later by email","Right after the trial session","Before the screening","Never, they will call"]},{"id":"d2q2","q":"What should you send the day before a trial?","options":["Nothing","A friendly reminder","A contract","An invoice"]},{"id":"d2q3","q":"A guest didn’t show up. What do you do?","options":["Nothing","Call the same day, friendly, and offer a new time","Block their number","Charge a fee"]},{"id":"d2q4","q":"How much of a good consultation is listening?","options":["About 10%","About 70%","0%","100% talking"]},{"id":"d2q5","q":"Which is an open question?","options":["“Do you like it?”","“What would you like to achieve in the next 3 months?”","“Yes or no?”","“Ready to sign?”"]},{"id":"d2q6","q":"Why repeat the guest’s goal in your own words?","options":["To fill time","To show you understood and to confirm the goal","To sell more","It’s not useful"]},{"id":"d2q7","q":"You recommend an option based on…","options":["the most expensive offer","the guest’s goal","your commission","random choice"]},{"id":"d2q8","q":"The guest needs time to think. What do you do?","options":["Push harder","Agree on a specific follow-up day and time","Let them go without anything","Offer a fake discount"]},{"id":"d2q9","q":"When should trials ideally be booked?","options":["Weeks away","Within the next few days","Only on Sundays","Doesn’t matter"]},{"id":"d2q10","q":"Where do prices and offers come from?","options":["Whatever feels right","The studio’s current offers and price list","Competitors","The guest decides"]}]},{"id":"watch","type":"video","title":"Watch & Explain","icon":"video","summary":"Leading a consultation.","videos":[{"id":"v-consult","title":"Leading a consultation","src":"","scenes":[{"type":"title","title":"Leading a consultation","caption":"Listen first, then recommend.","duration":5},{"type":"steps","title":"The trial journey","caption":"Book, remind, welcome, session, consultation.","items":["Book","Remind","Welcome","Session","Consult"],"duration":9},{"type":"chat","title":"Ask open questions","caption":"What made you look into EMS? What do you want to achieve? What stopped you before?","lines":[["coach","What would you like to achieve in 3 months?"],["client","Less back pain and feel stronger."]],"duration":10},{"type":"quote","title":"Repeat the goal","caption":"Show that you understood, then connect your recommendation to that goal.","text":"So you want to feel stronger without spending hours in the gym.","duration":8},{"type":"calendar","title":"Ask for the decision","caption":"“Shall we get your first sessions booked?” Or agree on a specific follow-up.","label":"Next session booked","duration":8}],"prompts":[{"id":"p1","question":"A guest just finished their free trial. Describe how you lead the consultation: which questions do you ask, and how do you get to a decision?","points":6}]}]}]},{"id":"desk-3","position":"desk","level":3,"title":"Objections & Retention","summary":"Handle concerns honestly, keep members engaged, and turn happy clients into referrals.","minutes":35,"modules":[{"id":"objections","title":"Handling Objections","icon":"chat","summary":"Turn concerns into honest conversations.","lessons":[{"id":"method","title":"The LAER method","html":"\n<p>An objection is usually a question in disguise. Use <strong>LAER</strong>:</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n  <li><strong>Listen</strong> fully, don’t interrupt.</li>\n  <li><strong>Acknowledge</strong>: <em>“I understand, that’s an important point.”</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Explore</strong>: ask what is behind it: <em>“What exactly worries you?”</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Respond</strong> honestly, linked to their goal.</li>\n</ol>"},{"id":"common","title":"Common objections","html":"\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>“It’s too expensive.”</strong> Explore: compared to what? Show the value: 20 minutes a week, personal coaching, time saved. Present the options honestly. Never invent discounts.</li>\n  <li><strong>“I don’t have time.”</strong> 20 minutes once a week is one of the most time-efficient workouts. Find a fixed weekly slot together.</li>\n  <li><strong>“Does it really work?”</strong> Ask how the trial felt. Explain that regular training over weeks brings results. No guarantees, no exaggerated claims.</li>\n  <li><strong>“Is it safe?”</strong> Health screening, certified coaches, one coach guiding the session, clear contraindications.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Honesty sells better than pressure. A client who feels respected comes back and brings friends.</div>"}]},{"id":"retention","title":"Retention & Referrals","icon":"heart","summary":"Keeping members happy and engaged.","lessons":[{"id":"signals","title":"Spotting members at risk","html":"\n<ul>\n  <li>Missed sessions two weeks in a row</li>\n  <li>Less enthusiasm, short answers, cancelling last minute</li>\n  <li>Membership end date approaching</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reach out early and personally: <em>“We missed you last week, everything okay? Shall I book your next session?”</em></p>"},{"id":"referrals","title":"Referrals","html":"\n<ul>\n  <li>Ask at the right moment: after a client shares a success or says they love it.</li>\n  <li><em>“Who do you know who would enjoy this too?”</em> Then offer to book a trial for that person.</li>\n  <li>Thank every referral personally.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"id":"check","title":"Final Check","icon":"check","summary":"Ten questions on Level 3.","lessons":[],"quiz":[{"id":"d3q1","q":"What does LAER stand for?","options":["Listen, Acknowledge, Explore, Respond","Look, Ask, Explain, Repeat","Learn, Answer, End, Remind","Listen, Argue, Explain, Repeat"]},{"id":"d3q2","q":"A guest says “It’s too expensive.” What is the best first step?","options":["Offer a made-up discount","Acknowledge and explore what they compare it to","Argue","End the conversation"]},{"id":"d3q3","q":"“I don’t have time.” What is a strong, honest response?","options":["“Then EMS isn’t for you.”","“It’s 20 minutes once a week – let’s find a fixed slot together.”","“Make time.”","“Train at home instead.”"]},{"id":"d3q4","q":"“Does it really work?” What should you avoid?","options":["Asking how the trial felt","Guarantees and exaggerated claims","Explaining regular training","Being honest"]},{"id":"d3q5","q":"“Is it safe?” Which points do you mention?","options":["Nothing","Health screening, certified coaches, guided sessions, contraindications","That everyone can train","That safety doesn’t matter"]},{"id":"d3q6","q":"Which is a warning sign that a member might quit?","options":["Booking the next session","Missing two weeks in a row","Referring a friend","Arriving early"]},{"id":"d3q7","q":"How do you reach out to a member at risk?","options":["A generic newsletter","Early and personally","Wait until they cancel","Not at all"]},{"id":"d3q8","q":"When is the best moment to ask for a referral?","options":["At the first phone call","After the client shares a success or says they love it","When they complain","Never"]},{"id":"d3q9","q":"What do you do after receiving a referral?","options":["Nothing","Thank the client personally","Charge a fee","Ignore it"]},{"id":"d3q10","q":"Why does honesty work better than pressure?","options":["It doesn’t","Respected clients come back and bring friends","It’s faster","It’s required by law"]}]},{"id":"watch","type":"video","title":"Watch & Explain","icon":"video","summary":"Handling the price objection.","videos":[{"id":"v-price","title":"“It’s too expensive”","src":"","scenes":[{"type":"title","title":"“It’s too expensive”","caption":"Turning an objection into a conversation.","duration":5},{"type":"steps","title":"LAER","caption":"Listen, Acknowledge, Explore, Respond.","items":["Listen","Acknowledge","Explore","Respond"],"duration":9},{"type":"chat","title":"Acknowledge and explore","caption":"Understand what the price is compared to.","lines":[["client","Honestly, it’s too expensive."],["coach","I understand. What are you comparing it to?"]],"duration":9},{"type":"stat","title":"Show the value","caption":"Personal coaching, 20 minutes a week, time saved, connected to their goal.","value":"20","unit":"min / week","duration":8},{"type":"quote","title":"Honest options","caption":"Present the official options and ask for a decision. Never invent discounts.","text":"Which option fits your goal and budget best?","duration":8}],"prompts":[{"id":"p1","question":"After the trial, a guest says: “I liked it, but it’s too expensive.” How do you respond? Write what you would say.","points":6}]}]}]},{"id":"mgr-1","position":"manager","level":1,"title":"Studio Operations","summary":"Daily routines, equipment and hygiene, and your responsibility for safety.","minutes":35,"modules":[{"id":"daily","title":"Daily Operations","icon":"calendar","summary":"Opening, running and closing the studio.","lessons":[{"id":"open-close","title":"Opening & closing","html":"\n<p><strong>Opening checklist</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"checklist\">\n  <li>Lights, music, temperature, clean entrance and mirrors</li>\n  <li>Stations powered on and working, cables undamaged</li>\n  <li>Clean, dry suits and enough functional underwear ready</li>\n  <li>Today’s bookings reviewed, first-time clients marked for screening</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Closing checklist</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"checklist\">\n  <li>Suits and electrodes cleaned as the manufacturer requires, hung to dry</li>\n  <li>Laundry started, stations switched off</li>\n  <li>Tomorrow’s schedule checked, open follow-ups assigned</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"equipment","title":"Equipment & hygiene","html":"\n<ul>\n  <li>Follow the <strong>manufacturer’s manual</strong> for cleaning, storage and maintenance.</li>\n  <li>Check suits regularly for wear: damaged electrodes or cables are taken out of use immediately.</li>\n  <li>Keep a simple <strong>maintenance log</strong>: what was checked, when, by whom.</li>\n  <li>Every client trains in clean functional underwear.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Clean, working equipment is both a safety standard and the first thing clients notice.</div>"}]},{"id":"safety","title":"Safety Responsibility","icon":"shield","summary":"Policies, incidents and documentation.","lessons":[{"id":"policies","title":"Your safety standards","html":"\n<p>As manager you make sure the studio’s safety rules are followed every day:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Only <strong>certified coaches</strong> who completed the academy run sessions alone.</li>\n  <li><strong>Health screening</strong> for every new client, updated when health changes.</li>\n  <li>The <strong>contraindication policy</strong> is applied consistently: when in doubt, no training.</li>\n  <li>A connected client is <strong>never left alone</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Emergency number (911), first-aid kit and the incident process are known by everyone.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"incidents","title":"Incident process","html":"\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n  <li><strong>Care first:</strong> stop the session, look after the client, call 911 if needed.</li>\n  <li><strong>Inform:</strong> the coach informs the manager immediately.</li>\n  <li><strong>Document</strong> the same day: who, what, when, programme and intensity, actions taken, witnesses.</li>\n  <li><strong>Follow up</strong> with the client personally. Training resumes only after a doctor’s clearance if needed.</li>\n  <li><strong>Learn:</strong> discuss with the team what could prevent it next time.</li>\n</ol>"}]},{"id":"check","title":"Final Check","icon":"check","summary":"Ten questions on Level 1.","lessons":[],"quiz":[{"id":"m1q1","q":"What do you check on stations when opening?","options":["Nothing","Powered on, working, cables undamaged","Only the screen colour","The music volume"]},{"id":"m1q2","q":"A suit has a damaged electrode. What do you do?","options":["Use it carefully","Take it out of use immediately","Tape it","Use it at low intensity"]},{"id":"m1q3","q":"Whose instructions apply for cleaning and maintenance?","options":["Your own","The manufacturer’s manual","The clients’","None"]},{"id":"m1q4","q":"What belongs in a maintenance log?","options":["Client names","What was checked, when and by whom","Prices","Nothing"]},{"id":"m1q5","q":"Who may run sessions alone?","options":["Anyone","Certified coaches who completed the academy","Front desk staff","Clients"]},{"id":"m1q6","q":"What is the first step in an incident?","options":["Write the report","Care for the client: stop, look after them, call 911 if needed","Call the owner","Continue the session"]},{"id":"m1q7","q":"When must an incident be documented?","options":["Within a month","The same day","Only if the client complains","Never"]},{"id":"m1q8","q":"What belongs in an incident report?","options":["Only the date","Who, what, when, programme and intensity, actions, witnesses","The client’s payment status","Nothing"]},{"id":"m1q9","q":"When does a client resume after a medical incident?","options":["Next day","After a doctor’s clearance if needed","Whenever they want","Never"]},{"id":"m1q10","q":"Why discuss incidents with the team?","options":["To blame someone","To learn how to prevent them","It’s not needed","For marketing"]}]},{"id":"watch","type":"video","title":"Watch & Explain","icon":"video","summary":"The incident process.","videos":[{"id":"v-incident","title":"The incident process","src":"","scenes":[{"type":"title","title":"The incident process","caption":"Care, inform, document, follow up, learn.","duration":5},{"type":"alert","title":"Care first","caption":"Stop the session, look after the client, call 911 if needed.","label":"Client first","duration":8},{"type":"steps","title":"Five steps","caption":"Care, inform, document, follow up, learn.","items":["Care","Inform","Document","Follow up","Learn"],"duration":10},{"type":"checklist","title":"Document the same day","caption":"Who, what, when, programme and intensity, actions taken, witnesses.","items":["Who & when","What happened","Programme & intensity","Actions & witnesses"],"duration":10}],"prompts":[{"id":"p1","question":"A client felt faint during a session today. Walk us through what you as manager make sure happens, today and in the following days.","points":6}]}]}]},{"id":"mgr-2","position":"manager","level":2,"title":"Leading the Team","summary":"Onboarding new staff, quality checks, and feedback that helps people grow.","minutes":35,"modules":[{"id":"onboarding","title":"Onboarding & Training","icon":"users","summary":"From first day to sign-off.","lessons":[{"id":"path","title":"The onboarding path","html":"\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n  <li><strong>Academy Level 1</strong> for the new position, with a one-time code from you.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shadowing:</strong> the new person watches several real sessions or desk shifts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Supervised practice:</strong> they lead, an experienced colleague observes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sign-off:</strong> you confirm they are ready, and document it.</li>\n  <li><strong>Next levels</strong> after a few weeks of practice.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Knowledge from the academy plus observed practice equals a confident, safe team.</div>"},{"id":"using-academy","title":"Using the academy","html":"\n<ul>\n  <li>Create one code per person and course in the admin area. The code expires when the course is finished.</li>\n  <li>Check the written and spoken answers. Automatic scores are a first indication; you can override them.</li>\n  <li>Use weak answers as a starting point for a personal coaching conversation.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"id":"quality","title":"Quality & Feedback","icon":"star","summary":"Session checks and feedback that works.","lessons":[{"id":"audits","title":"Quality checks","html":"\n<p>Observe each coach regularly, for example once a month, with a short checklist:</p>\n<ul class=\"checklist\">\n  <li>Health check and preparation done</li>\n  <li>Intensity raised gradually and in dialogue</li>\n  <li>Clear cues, good technique, positive energy</li>\n  <li>Client never left alone</li>\n  <li>Session documented, equipment cleaned</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"feedback","title":"Feedback with SBI","html":"\n<p>Use <strong>SBI</strong>: Situation, Behaviour, Impact. Then agree on the next step.</p>\n<div class=\"script\">“In your 10 am session today (situation), you left the client connected while you answered the phone (behaviour). That puts the client at risk and breaks our safety rule (impact). What can you do next time when the phone rings?”</div>\n<ul>\n  <li>Give feedback <strong>soon and in private</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Describe facts, not personality.</li>\n  <li>Praise in public, correct in private.</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"id":"check","title":"Final Check","icon":"check","summary":"Ten questions on Level 2.","lessons":[],"quiz":[{"id":"m2q1","q":"What comes right after Academy Level 1 in onboarding?","options":["Sign-off","Shadowing real sessions","Level 3","Nothing"]},{"id":"m2q2","q":"What is supervised practice?","options":["Watching videos","The new person leads, an experienced colleague observes","Training alone","A written test"]},{"id":"m2q3","q":"When does a code expire?","options":["After one day","When the course is finished","Never","After one login"]},{"id":"m2q4","q":"How do you treat the automatic answer scores?","options":["As final","As a first indication you can override","Ignore them","Delete them"]},{"id":"m2q5","q":"How often should coaches be observed?","options":["Never","Regularly, for example once a month","Once a year","Only after complaints"]},{"id":"m2q6","q":"What does SBI stand for?","options":["Situation, Behaviour, Impact","Speak, Blame, Improve","Start, Build, Increase","Safety, Balance, Intensity"]},{"id":"m2q7","q":"Where should corrective feedback be given?","options":["In front of the team","In private","By group chat","In front of clients"]},{"id":"m2q8","q":"Good feedback describes…","options":["personality","facts and behaviour","rumours","feelings only"]},{"id":"m2q9","q":"When should feedback be given?","options":["Soon after the situation","At the yearly review","Never","Only in writing"]},{"id":"m2q10","q":"What should every feedback conversation end with?","options":["A warning","An agreed next step","Nothing","A pay cut"]}]},{"id":"watch","type":"video","title":"Watch & Explain","icon":"video","summary":"Giving difficult feedback.","videos":[{"id":"v-feedback","title":"Feedback that works","src":"","scenes":[{"type":"title","title":"Feedback that works","caption":"Situation, behaviour, impact.","duration":5},{"type":"alert","title":"What you saw","caption":"A coach left a connected client alone to answer the phone.","label":"Safety rule broken","duration":8},{"type":"steps","title":"SBI","caption":"Describe the situation, the behaviour, the impact. Then agree on the next step.","items":["Situation","Behaviour","Impact","Next step"],"duration":10},{"type":"chat","title":"In private, soon","caption":"Facts, not personality. Ask for their solution.","lines":[["coach","What can you do next time when the phone rings?"],["client","Let it ring or ask the front desk."]],"duration":9}],"prompts":[{"id":"p1","question":"You saw a coach leave a connected client alone to answer the phone. Write the feedback conversation you would have with the coach.","points":6}]}]}]},{"id":"mgr-3","position":"manager","level":3,"title":"Business & Growth","summary":"The numbers that matter, and how to grow the studio in the community.","minutes":40,"modules":[{"id":"numbers","title":"Numbers That Matter","icon":"chart","summary":"Five key figures and how to read them.","lessons":[{"id":"kpis","title":"Five key figures","html":"\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Leads:</strong> new inquiries per week (calls, website, Instagram, walk-ins).</li>\n  <li><strong>Trial show-up rate</strong> = trials attended ÷ trials booked.</li>\n  <li><strong>Conversion rate</strong> = new members ÷ trials attended.</li>\n  <li><strong>Retention</strong> = members still active after 3 / 6 months ÷ members who started then.</li>\n  <li><strong>Utilisation</strong> = booked sessions ÷ available session slots.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> 20 trials booked, 16 attended, 8 joined. Show-up rate = 16 ÷ 20 = <strong>80%</strong>. Conversion = 8 ÷ 16 = <strong>50%</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"reading","title":"Reading the numbers","html":"\n<p>Each number points to a different part of the journey:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Few leads → marketing and visibility.</li>\n  <li>Low show-up → booking speed, reminders, follow-up.</li>\n  <li>Low conversion → trial experience and consultation.</li>\n  <li>Low retention → session quality, progress check-ins, personal contact.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"takeaway\">Don’t guess. Find the step where people drop off, then fix that step.</div>"}]},{"id":"growth","title":"Local Growth","icon":"star","summary":"Visibility and community.","lessons":[{"id":"local","title":"Growing locally","html":"\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Google Business profile and reviews:</strong> ask happy members for honest reviews.</li>\n  <li><strong>Instagram:</strong> real people, real sessions (with consent), short tips, behind the scenes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Partnerships:</strong> local businesses, physiotherapists, offices nearby.</li>\n  <li><strong>Referrals:</strong> make it easy for members to bring friends to a trial.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All marketing must be honest: no fake reviews, no medical promises, no claims the studio can’t support.</p>"}]},{"id":"check","title":"Final Check","icon":"check","summary":"Ten questions on Level 3.","lessons":[],"quiz":[{"id":"m3q1","q":"How is the conversion rate calculated?","options":["New members ÷ trials attended","Trials booked ÷ leads","Sessions ÷ slots","Members ÷ coaches"]},{"id":"m3q2","q":"20 trials booked, 16 attended. What is the show-up rate?","options":["16%","80%","125%","50%"]},{"id":"m3q3","q":"16 trials attended, 8 joined. What is the conversion rate?","options":["8%","50%","200%","16%"]},{"id":"m3q4","q":"What does utilisation measure?","options":["Booked sessions ÷ available slots","Revenue per month","Number of coaches","Instagram followers"]},{"id":"m3q5","q":"Show-up rate is low. Where do you look first?","options":["Marketing budget","Booking speed, reminders and follow-up","Coach uniforms","Prices"]},{"id":"m3q6","q":"Conversion is low. Where do you look first?","options":["Trial experience and consultation","The parking lot","Opening hours","Logo"]},{"id":"m3q7","q":"Retention is low. What do you check?","options":["Session quality, progress check-ins, personal contact","Only prices","Only marketing","Nothing"]},{"id":"m3q8","q":"Which marketing practice is NOT acceptable?","options":["Asking happy members for honest reviews","Writing fake reviews","Partnering with local businesses","Posting real sessions with consent"]},{"id":"m3q9","q":"What do you need before posting a member in a photo?","options":["Nothing","Their consent","A coach’s approval","A paid membership"]},{"id":"m3q10","q":"What is the main rule for fixing a numbers problem?","options":["Guess and change everything","Find the step where people drop off and fix that step","Lower prices","Hire more staff"]}]},{"id":"watch","type":"video","title":"Watch & Explain","icon":"video","summary":"Analysing a drop in conversion.","videos":[{"id":"v-funnel","title":"Reading the funnel","src":"","scenes":[{"type":"title","title":"Reading the funnel","caption":"Where do people drop off?","duration":5},{"type":"funnel","title":"The member journey","caption":"Leads, trials booked, trials attended, new members.","steps":[["Leads",100],["Trials booked",40],["Attended",32],["Joined",16]],"duration":10},{"type":"bars","title":"Conversion dropped","caption":"Last quarter 50%, this quarter 30%. The problem is between trial and decision.","bars":[["Q1",50],["Q2",48],["Q3",30]],"unit":"%","duration":9},{"type":"checklist","title":"Look at that step","caption":"Trial experience, consultation quality, follow-up, who led the trials.","items":["Trial experience","Consultation","Follow-up","Who led the trials"],"duration":10}],"prompts":[{"id":"p1","question":"Your conversion rate (new members ÷ trials attended) dropped from 50% to 30% this quarter. What do you analyse, and what could you do?","points":6}]}]}]}]};
